.\" Man page for swimflow. -*- nroff -*-
.TH SWIMFLOW 1 "2025" "swimflow" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
swimflow \- generate activity/swimlane, sequence, tree, treemap and manifest \
diagrams from a text DSL
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B swimflow
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B swimflow
turns a small text DSL into diagrams. It reads a diagram specification and
writes the rendered diagram. The canonical output is a standalone SVG; PNG and
PDF are derived from that same SVG, and an interactive HTML bundle can inline
several diagrams with drill-down navigation.
.PP
The
.I .diag
language is a small DOT-like language, compatible with the reference
implementation.
.PP
Five diagram types are supported. Each one is a plugin: it owns the reading of
the source and the drawing, while fonts, PNG/PDF conversion and the HTML
bundle are shared. The type is detected from the file extension
.RB ( .diag ", " .seq ", " .tree ", " .tmap ", " .mfst )
or from the content of the file, and can be selected explicitly with
.BR \-\-type .
Run
.B swimflow \-\-list\-types
for the list.
.PP
If
.I file
is omitted or is
.BR \- ,
the specification is read from standard input. If
.B \-o
is omitted and the input is a file, output is written next to it with the
extension implied by the chosen format.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a help message and exit. The short
.B \-h
form gives a one-line summary per option; the long
.B \-\-help
form adds the full descriptions, headings and examples. Both wrap to the
terminal width.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type " " \fITYPE\fR
Select the diagram type of the input:
.B activity
(swimlane diagrams, reference compatible) or
.B sequence
(sequence diagrams), or
.B tree
(tree diagrams), or
.B treemap
(nested rectangles sized by a number) or
.B manifest
(which rows belong to which columns). The type is normally detected
automatically, so this option is only needed for files with an unusual
extension or for standard input.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-types
Print the available diagram types and their descriptions, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIPATH\fR
Write output to
.IR PATH .
The special value
.B \-
writes to standard output. When omitted, the format is inferred from the
extension of
.IR PATH .
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fIformat\fR
Select the output format:
.BR svg ,
.BR png ,
.B pdf
or
.BR html .
Defaults to
.B svg
(and to
.B html
when
.B \-\-bundle
is given).
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bundle " " \fIPATH\fR
Emit one self-contained interactive HTML file containing every diagram in the
loaded document set. Nodes with a
.B ref
attribute navigate to the referenced diagram; a breadcrumb, back button, URL
hash routing and the Escape key are provided by an inline, dependency-free
runtime. The diagram opens fitted to the window; the bar has zoom buttons, and
Ctrl or Cmd with the mouse wheel zooms around the pointer (the keys
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
.B 1
(actual size) and
.B 0
(fit) work too). Zoom affects the page only, never an exported file.
.TP
.BR \-\-root " " \fIDIAGRAM_ID\fR
Choose the entry diagram for the HTML bundle. Defaults to the first diagram of
the root document.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-external
Do not emit external
.B href
links in the output. Internal
.B ref
links are unaffected.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-external\-files
Do not load files referenced by
.BR "ref = \[dq]other.diag#..." ;
only references within the input document resolve.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-broken\-links
Keep going when a
.B ref
target cannot be resolved: the node is rendered disabled and a warning is
printed, instead of failing.
.TP
.B \-\-embed\-font
Embed the selected font as a base64
.B @font-face
inside the SVG (or, for a bundle, once in the page CSS) so the output is fully
self-contained.
.TP
.B \-\-text\-as\-paths
Render text as vector outlines. The result contains no
.B <text>
elements and has no font dependency.
.TP
.BR \-\-font " " \fIpath\fR
Measure, embed and rasterize text with the TrueType/OpenType font at
.IR path ,
instead of the built-in VL Gothic. The font's declared family name is used for
the SVG
.B @font-face
and
.B font-family
attributes and for the
.B usvg
database behind PNG and PDF. Layout is font-independent, so box geometry does
not change.
.TP
.BR \-\-font\-family " " \fIname\fR
Look up the installed font family
.I name
through fontconfig and use it for text measurement and rendering. Ignored when
.B \-\-font
is given. Layout is font-independent.
.TP
.B \-\-nodoctype
Omit the XML doctype declaration from SVG output.
.TP
.B \-\-noviewbox
Emit
.B width
and
.B height
instead of a
.B viewBox
on the root SVG element.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-transparent
Do not paint an opaque background.
.TP
.BR \-\-scale " " \fIfactor\fR
Raster scale for PNG output (1.0 = 96 DPI). The live-preview server uses
.B SWIMFLOW_SCALE
instead.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-antialias
Render PNG at twice the size, for compatibility with the reference
.B \-\-antialias
option. In the live-preview server, set
.B SWIMFLOW_SCALE=2.0
instead.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-ast
Parse the input, print the syntax tree and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-model
Build the model, print lanes, nodes and edges, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-geometry
Build and lay out the model, then print grid geometry as JSON and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-pixels
With
.BR \-\-dump\-geometry ,
print pixel boxes instead of integer grid cells.
.TP
.B \-\-serve
Run an HTTP server that lists the
.I .diag
files under a configured directory and renders an editable live preview in
the browser. Configuration is read from a
.I .env
file; see
.B ENVIRONMENT
below and
.IR \.env.example .
The server binds all interfaces by default so it can be reached from other
machines.
.TP
.BR \-\-env\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
Read server configuration from
.I PATH
instead of
.IR \./.env .
A missing file is an error. The
.B SWIMFLOW_ENV
environment variable is an alternative way to select the file.
.PP
The options grouped under
.B CLI only
in
.B swimflow \-h
are one-shot settings: they affect a direct render but are ignored by
.BR \-\-serve ,
which uses its own defaults. They are
.BR \-\-root ,
.BR \-\-no\-external ,
.BR \-\-no\-external\-files ,
.BR \-\-allow\-broken\-links ,
.BR \-\-embed\-font ,
.BR \-\-text\-as\-paths ,
.BR \-\-nodoctype ,
.BR \-\-noviewbox
and
.BR \-\-transparent .
The server settings it does understand are listed under
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.SH DIAGRAM LANGUAGE
The activity diagram language is described in
.IR docs/dsl.rst
in the source distribution. In brief:
.PP
.nf
diagram overview {
  Start -> Review;
  Review [ref = "#detail", label = "Review"];
}
diagram detail {
  lane "CI" { Build -> Test; }
}
.fi
.PP
Attribute statements inside
.B [ ... ]
carry
.BR label ,
.BR color ,
.BR shape ,
.BR ref ,
.BR href ,
.B numbered
and other attributes. Unknown attributes are preserved. A
.B ref
value may be
.IR detail ,
.IR "#detail" ,
.IR other.diag
or
.IR other.diag#detail .
.PP
The sequence diagram language looks like this:
.PP
.nf
sequence "Checkout" {
  participant "Client" as client;
  participant "Server";

  client -> Server: "POST /orders";
  Server --> client: "201 Created";

  note over client, Server: "the request phase";

  loop "until confirmed" {
    Server -> client: "still there?";
  }
}
.fi
.PP
The message operators are
.B \-> (a synchronous call),
.B \->> (an asynchronous message),
.B \-\-> (a reply) and
.B \-\- (a result). Frames are introduced by
.BR loop ,
.BR opt ,
.BR par ,
.BR alt ,
.BR critical ,
.BR break ,
.B else
and
.BR and .
The tree diagram language looks like this:
.PP
.nf
tree "Product" {
  root "Swimflow";

  "Core" {
    "Parser";
    "Renderer" { "SVG"; "PNG"; }
  }
  "Docs" [color = "#cfe8ff"];
}
.fi
.PP
A node is a name with optional attributes, followed by either
.B ;
(a leaf) or a block of children. The attributes are
.BR label ,
.BR color ,
.BR textcolor ,
.BR linecolor ,
.BR shape ,
.BR style ,
.BR href ,
.B target
(where an \fBhref\fR opens, as in HTML: \fB_blank\fR for a new tab),
.B edge
(a label on the connector into the node),
.B edgestyle
(\fBdashed\fR draws that connector dashed, except where it merges with a solid one),
.B collapsed
(start with the children folded away) and
.B ref
(open another tree of the same file). A circle on every branch (on its bottom edge, or on its right edge in
landscape) folds and
unfolds it: a minus while the children are shown, a plus while they are
folded. The circles work in an HTML bundle and in the live preview, where
.B Collapse all
and
.B Expand all
buttons in the top bar fold or unfold every branch at once; SVG, PNG
and PDF written by the command line show the initial state, while the
.B Download
button of the live preview writes the view on screen, folds included.
.B orientation = landscape;
makes the tree grow from left to right.
.PP
The treemap language looks like this:
.PP
.nf
treemap "Budget" {
  class housing [color = "#cfe8ff"];

  root "Monthly budget";

  Housing: 1500 [class = housing] {
    Rent: 1000 [class = housing];
    Utilities: 320 [class = housing];
  }
  Food: 620;
  Savings: 500;
}
.fi
.PP
The number follows the name after a colon and decides the area. A leaf without
a number counts 1; a container without one uses the sum of its children. An
explicit container value only decides its own share among its siblings. The
node attributes are
.BR label ,
.BR color ,
.BR textcolor ,
.BR linecolor ,
.B href
and
.B target
and
.BR showvalue .
A
.B class NAME [attrs];
statement defines reusable attributes, applied with
.B [class = NAME]
(the node's own attributes win). Values are drawn by default. In an HTML bundle a
.B Hide values / Show values
button in the top bar toggles them; SVG, PNG and PDF have no button and
show the values the source asks for. The diagram attributes
.B width
and
.B height
set the canvas (default 800 by 500 pixels). Labels that do not fit are
truncated with three dots. The core zoom works on a treemap like on every
other diagram type.
.PP
The manifest language looks like this:
.PP
.nf
manifest "Products" {
  group "Line" {
    column base { core; ui; net; }
    column lite [extends = base] { remove net; }
    column pro [extends = base] { override ui [note = "v2"]; cloud; }
  }
  row docs: base, pro;
}
.fi
.PP
A column is a set and a row an element: a row listed in a column block, or
naming the column after a colon in a
.B row
statement, is a member (a filled dot). A column with
.B extends
inherits the rows of another column (hollow dots), transitively;
.B remove
drops an inherited row (a grey cross) and
.B override
keeps it but marks it changed (a ringed dot).
.B group
draws a band over several columns and
.B rowgroup
a titled block of rows. A count column shows how many columns each row is in;
rows are sorted by it unless
.B sort
is
.B name
or
.BR source .
The diagram attributes are
.BR title ,
.BR sort ,
.BR showcount ,
.B countlabel
and
.BR legend ;
columns, rows and groups take
.BR label ,
.BR color ,
.B textcolor
and
.B href
(columns and rows also
.BR markcolor ;
a group also
.B shape
=
.B roundedbox
for rounded corners),
and an entry takes a
.B note
shown in its tooltip. In an HTML bundle hovering a cell highlights its row and
column; the cross next to a row or column hides it, a legend entry hides every
mark of its kind, clicking a column header shows only that column's rows, and clicking a row
label shows only that row's columns; clicking a cell does both.
A hidden row or column folds to a thin strip with a
.B +
that unfolds it, and a download from the live preview leaves it out.
.B hidemarks
lists the kinds of mark hidden at first (default
.BR removed ;
.B none
shows them all).
.SH EXAMPLES
The source distribution ships an
.I examples/
directory with runnable diagrams for basic flows, swimlanes, CI/CD, incident
response, state machines, dependency graphs, unicode and drill-down. See
.I examples/README.rst
for the full tour. Some starting points:
.PP
.nf
    swimflow examples/hello.diag
    swimflow examples/deployment.diag -f png --scale 2 -o deployment.png
    swimflow examples/zoom-demo.diag --bundle zoom-demo.html --embed-font
.fi
.PP
Render the default diagram as SVG (writes
.IR simple.svg ):
.PP
.nf
    swimflow simple.diag
.fi
.PP
Write PNG at 2x scale:
.PP
.nf
    swimflow simple.diag -f png --scale 2 -o simple.png
.fi
.PP
Build a self-contained interactive bundle from a multi-diagram file:
.PP
.nf
    swimflow manual.diag --bundle manual.html --embed-font
.fi
.PP
Read from standard input and write SVG to standard output:
.PP
.nf
    echo '{ A -> B; }' | swimflow -o -
.fi
.PP
Render a sequence diagram:
.PP
.nf
    swimflow examples/sequence/checkout.seq
    swimflow examples/sequence/checkout.seq -f png -o checkout.png
.fi
.PP
Render a tree diagram:
.PP
.nf
    swimflow examples/tree/org-chart.tree
    swimflow examples/tree/file-system.tree -f png -o layout.png
.fi
.PP
Render a treemap:
.PP
.nf
    swimflow examples/treemap/budget.tmap
    swimflow examples/treemap/disk-usage.tmap -f png -o disk-usage.png
.fi
.PP
Render a manifest:
.PP
.nf
    swimflow examples/manifest/editions.mfst
    swimflow examples/manifest/editions.mfst --bundle editions.html
.fi
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.B swimflow
accepts the same
.I .diag
files as the reference implementation and targets the same integer-grid
layout and pixel metrics.
New syntax (multiple diagrams,
.BR ref ,
legends, sequence numbers) is additive: existing files are unaffected.
The docutils/Sphinx directive options
.BR :width: ,
.B :height:
and
.B :scale:
are not supported, because
.B swimflow
is a standalone binary rather than a Sphinx extension; use
.B \-\-scale
for raster scaling.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The live-preview server (
.BR \-\-serve )
reads a small
.I .env
file (default
.IR \./.env ,
optional) and then lets the process environment override it. Recognised keys:
.TP
.B SWIMFLOW_DIR
The directory scanned recursively for diagram files (default: the working
directory). Only files of a known diagram type below it are served:
.I .diag
(activity/swimlane),
.I .seq
(sequence),
.I .tree
(tree),
.I .tmap
(treemap) and
.I .mfst
(manifest).
.TP
.B SWIMFLOW_HOST
The interface to bind (default
.BR 0.0.0.0 ,
reachable from other machines).
.TP
.B SWIMFLOW_PORT
The TCP port (default
.BR 8000 ).
.TP
.B SWIMFLOW_DEBOUNCE_MS
How long to wait after the last keystroke before re-rendering the preview
(default
.BR 600 ).
.TP
.B SWIMFLOW_SCALE
Raster scale for PNG downloads (default
.BR 1.0 ,
where 1.0 is 96 DPI). Must be a positive finite number; 2 doubles the pixel
size. This is the live-preview equivalent of
.BR \-\-scale .
.TP
.B SWIMFLOW_TOKEN
Optional shared secret. When set, the page must be opened with
.BR ?token= \fIvalue\fR;
a cookie then keeps the session authorised.
.PP
In the browser the diagrams appear in a tree; selecting one shows its source
next to the rendered HTML bundle (drill-down links work). Editing the source
re-renders after the debounce delay. The server never writes to the source
files: the
.B Download
button (or Ctrl+S) exports the current editor contents to a new file.
The
.B Expand
button makes the rendered diagram fill the window below the toolbar, hiding
the file list and editor; press Escape or
.B Restore
to bring them back.
.PP
The sidebar has one tab per diagram type. The tab selects the files that are
listed, the syntax highlighting, the starter template of a new file and the
export formats; everything else works the same for every type.
.PP
The sidebar can also import diagram files from the local disk and create new
diagrams from scratch. Those buffers live in the browser tab only; they are
never written to the served directory and they are gone when the tab is
closed.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
A bash completion file ships in the source distribution at
.IR completions/swimflow.bash .
Source it for the current shell, or install it where bash-completion looks for
it:
.PP
.nf
    source completions/swimflow.bash
    cp completions/swimflow.bash \e
        ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/swimflow
.fi
